 ### Bradford Chromatic Adaptation for White Balance
-A post-processing white balance technique that uses the Bradford chromatic adaptation transform to map colors from a Planck/CIE-daylight illuminant to D65 [S2, unofficial]. Unlike simple gray-world or white-patch algorithms, it explicitly models the chromatic adaptation process, providing a more physically grounded correction for white balance in rendering pipelines [S2, unofficial].
+A post-processing white balance technique using Bradford chromatic adaptation to map from a Planck/CIE-daylight illuminant to D65 [S2, unofficial]. It applies the Bradford transform directly to the scene-linear image before tone mapping [S2, unofficial]. It differs from simple gray-world or white-patch algorithms by using a physically-based chromatic adaptation transform [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per image, where $N$ is the number of pixels, dominated by the least squares solve and polynomial application [G].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the feature matrix and intermediate arrays [G].
-**Strengths:** Effectively neutralizes global brightness and color shifts caused by autoexposure and white balance variations, allowing fair comparison between models [S1].
-**Limitations:** Does not correct for local photometric inconsistencies or tone-mapping differences; primarily designed for evaluation rather than training [S1].
-**When to use:** Evaluating view synthesis on datasets with uncontrolled camera settings where autoexposure/white balance varies between images [S1].
-**When not to use:** Training neural fields, as the per-image least squares solve is computationally expensive and breaks gradient flow through the metric [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per image for the least squares fit, where $N$ is the number of valid pixels. The fit is solved once per image.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store pixel values during the fit.
+- **Strengths:** Mitigates the impact of autoexposure and white balance variations in uncontrolled datasets [S1]. Makes models like SVS and GLO quantitatively comparable [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Does not correct for spatially varying lighting or shadows, only global photometric shifts [S1]. Computationally adds overhead to evaluation [G].
+- **When to use:** Evaluating view synthesis on "in the wild" datasets like Tanks and Temples where camera settings are not fixed [S1].
+- **When not to use:** On controlled datasets with fixed photometry, or when spatially varying color errors are of interest [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the context of evaluating Neural Radiance Fields on the Tanks and Temples dataset, where photometric variation makes standard evaluation metrics ill-posed [S1]. It builds on earlier work by Martin-Brualla et al. who investigated specialized training and evaluation procedures for such datasets [S1]. The approach was used to compare the Mip-NeRF 360 model with Stable View Synthesis (SVS) [S1].
+Originated in the context of evaluating neural radiance fields on unbounded, "in the wild" datasets [S1]. The challenge was identified as photometric variation (autoexposure, white balance, clipping) making evaluation ill-posed [S1]. The method was proposed to neutralize these variations during evaluation, allowing for fair comparison between different view synthesis models [S1]. It is distinct from training-time corrections, focusing solely on the evaluation phase [S1].
 
